--- !u!114 &1247866449
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1247866447}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: c67f774ab82d7e24889a72ca39fd0d98, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  title: 'Shift 4: Debrief'
  concept: 'Time to check what you learned. The session layer:


    - <b>establishes</b>
    sessions (request, authenticate, negotiate, confirm with a session ID)

    -
    <b>manages the dialog</b> (simplex, half-duplex with a token, full-duplex)

    -
    <b>synchronizes</b> with checkpoints so failures don''t mean starting over

    -
    <b>terminates</b> sessions in an orderly way'
  howToPlay: Five questions. Pick the best answer; an explanation appears after each
    one.
  lesson: 'The session layer is the conversation manager: it opens, controls, checkpoints
    and closes the dialog between two applications, while the layers below it just
    move the bits.'
  feedbackText: {fileID: 0}
  questions:
  - text: Where does the session layer sit in the OSI model?
    options:
    - Layer 4, below Transport
    - Layer 5, between Transport and Presentation
    - Layer 6, between Presentation and Application
    - Layer 3, alongside Network
    correct: 1
    why: Session is Layer 5. It sits on top of Transport (4), which moves the bytes,
      and below Presentation (6), which formats them.
  - text: In half-duplex dialog control, how does the session layer stop both sides
      from talking at once?
    options:
    - Only the side holding the token may transmit
    - Both transmit and the stronger signal wins
    - It adds MAC addresses to each frame
    - It encrypts every message
    correct: 0
    why: A token grants the right to send. Passing it hands the turn to the other
      side.
  - text: A 500 MB transfer has sync points every 50 MB. The link drops at 430 MB.
      Where does it resume?
    options:
    - '0 MB: start over'
    - '430 MB: exactly where it dropped'
    - '400 MB: the last checkpoint'
    - '450 MB: the next checkpoint'
    correct: 2
    why: Both sides only agreed on data up to the last sync point (400 MB), so that
      is where the session resynchronizes.
  - text: Why does the server give each session its own ID?
    options:
    - To pick the fastest route across the internet
    - To compress the data
    - To convert data into a common format
    - To keep each conversation's exchanges separate, even between the same two hosts
    correct: 3
    why: The session ID tags every exchange so many sessions can run side by side.
      Routing is Layer 3; compression and formatting are Layer 6.
  - text: What is the advantage of a graceful release over just dropping the connection?
    options:
    - It's faster
    - Both sides confirm they're done, so no data in flight is lost
    - It skips authentication next time
    - It upgrades the session to full-duplex
    correct: 1
    why: An orderly release is a two-way agreement to stop, so neither side is left
      wondering if the last data arrived.
  counterText: {fileID: 134585611}
  questionText: {fileID: 1696302890}
  optionButtons:
  - {fileID: 474733957}
  - {fileID: 1909858831}
  - {fileID: 1232064872}
  - {fileID: 114665090}
  verdictText: {fileID: 1216785707}
  whyText: {fileID: 43385248}
  nextButton: {fileID: 331592040}
